notes:
[round 1] Round 1 — GKR-100 construction record.
Panel results are blind during construction, so selection is based on item inspection against the construct (retrieval-verifiable, single, dated, citable answer) plus judged difficulty.
Process: screened ~150 candidates across all 10 fields (full text + key reviewed for every candidate). 
Exclusion criteria applied:
 (a) questionable or over-specific keys for exact matching (e.g., F0045 key with honorific 'Shri', F0097/F0574 long full names with titles, F0214 spelling variant 'Meher Ali', F0028 range format, F0239 unordered two-name key);
 (b) ambiguous/relative wording (F0053 'created' vs launched, F0862 PS5 'announced', F0185 'most listened-to', F0540 'find the town', F0130 passengers vs total deaths);
 (c) trivial items any system answers (F0301 Taj Mahal, F0198 Eddard Stark, F0133 India World Cups, F0087 LHC 3.5 TeV, F0141 Perceval, F0117 Jinnah, F0047 Obama, F0138 '3437 Kapitsa' guessable from stem);
 (d) template duplicates (Colombian municipality founding years—kept none).
Coverage target (approximately proportional to pool, with small fields given a floor): Sci&Tech 18, Politics 15, Art 14, Geography 11, Sports 10, Music 9, Other 9, TV 7, History 4, Video games 3. Rationale: pool proportions reflect the source distribution of citable-fact questions; the three largest fields carry most of the discriminative load, while every field is represented so the score is not driven by one domain's niche (e.g. Dark Souls/Terraria patch notes dominate Video games, so only 3 non-patch-note VG items kept).
Difficulty mix: mostly mid/hard long-tail facts (award recipients by year, dates of obscure events, named people), with a few easier anchors (F0656 Paulus quote, F0480 Dennis Weaver, F0516 Harmony Cobel, F0085 Jason Statham) to separate weak from mid systems.

[round 1] Round 1 decisions.
Construct: retrieval-verifiable factual knowledge with single, dated, citable answers; exact-match grading.
Screening (192 candidates viewed in full -> 100 kept). Exclusion criteria applied:
 (a) multi-part keys (two names, year ranges) that are fragile under exact match (e.g., F0043, F0028, F0239);
 (b) keys with honorifics/extra tokens or unusual formatting (e.g., "Shri H. D. Deve Gowda", "18 episodes", "Rev. Randy McCain", "9, September 2016");
 (c) ambiguous referents / contestable premises (first Prime Minister of "Congo"; "man who came up with the idea of the iPhone"; "who played the vampire"; PS5 "announce" date; "female disc jockey" for an equestrian; "total passengers" when the key counts all fatalities);
 (d) trivially easy items (Taj Mahal, Oxford for Bhutto, Obama award) that would not discriminate;
 (e) first-person/temporal wording ("my camera", "as of 2021").
Edits (4): expanded abbreviations (MKA -> Mustafa Kemal Atatürk; ARA -> American Relief Administration), disambiguated "gave the slogan" -> "coined the slogan" (Quit India, key = Yusuf Meher Ali), rewrote awkward Munch item.
Coverage target (roughly proportional to pool size, with a floor for small fields): Sci&Tech 20, Politics 16, Art 13, Geography 9, History 8, Music 9, Sports 9, Other 6, TV 6, Video games 4.
Panel check on the 100: accuracy range 36%–68% across 13 systems, monotone separation between tiers; no ceiling or floor effects. Panel only returns aggregates, so item-level discrimination is managed by content judgment (obscure-but-citable facts: award recipients by year, dated events, named individuals), not by per-item stats.
